    "slug": "eucap-somalia-concludes-isps-code-training-for-puntland-port-security",
    "summary": "EUCAP Somalia delivered a five-day training on the ISPS Code to enhance port security capacity in Puntland, concluding in Bossaso.",
    "content": "The European Union Capacity Building Mission in Somalia (EUCAP Somalia) has completed a five-day training programme on the International Ship and Port Facility Security (ISPS) Code in Bossaso, Puntland. The training, conducted through EUCAP's Puntland Field Office, aimed to strengthen maritime security capacity and improve port safety in the region. Participants included port security personnel and officials responsible for implementing the ISPS Code, an international framework designed to prevent security incidents at ports and on ships.\n\nThe ISPS Code, adopted by the International Maritime Organization (IMO) in 2002, sets standards for security at port facilities and on vessels engaged in international trade. For Puntland, which has key ports such as Bossaso and Berbera, effective implementation of the code is essential for ensuring the safety of maritime operations and facilitating legitimate trade. The training likely covered risk assessment, security planning, and emergency response procedures, though specific details were not provided.\n\nEUCAP Somalia is a civilian mission under the EU's Common Security and Defence Policy, focused on building the capacity of Somali maritime security forces and institutions. The mission has been operating since 2012 and works closely with Somali authorities to enhance maritime governance, law enforcement, and port security. This training is part of ongoing efforts to support Puntland's ability to manage its ports in line with international standards.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, improved port security in Puntland is significant. Many diaspora members maintain business ties and investments in the region, particularly in the shipping and logistics sectors. Enhanced port security can facilitate smoother trade flows, reduce risks associated with maritime crime, and boost confidence among international partners and investors. Additionally, safer ports contribute to overall stability, which is a key concern for diaspora communities considering return or investment.\n\nThe training also underscores the continued engagement of international partners in Somalia's maritime security. As Puntland seeks to develop its port infrastructure and attract foreign investment, adherence to international security standards like the ISPS Code is crucial. The EU's support through EUCAP Somalia aligns with broader efforts to promote maritime security and economic development in the region.\n\nRead the original report from Horseed Media here: https://horseedmedia.net/eucap-somalia-strengthens-port-security-capacity-in-puntland-through-isps-code-training/428046/",
